     "z": 0.009783,
     "z_err": null,
     "mu": 33.048,
     "cosmology": null,
     "source": "NGC 4993, from a distance indicator rather than from the redshift.  Adopted value is the surface brightness fluctuation distance: Cantiello et al. 2018, ApJL 854, L31, DOI 10.3847/2041-8213/aaad64, \"For the preferred F110W passband we derive a distance modulus of (m - M) = 33.05 +- 0.08 +- 0.10 mag, or a linear distance d = 40.7 +- 1.4 +- 1.9 Mpc (random and systematic errors, respectively)\".  d_L_err_Mpc is the two terms added in quadrature, sqrt(1.4^2 + 1.9^2) = 2.3601 Mpc, quoted here as 2.36 because the sources print one significant figure in each term and more digits would be a false claim about how well the error is known.  The combination matters because `Distance` carries one error and quoting the systematic term alone would drop the random one and understate the budget by 0.025 mag of distance modulus; the two components are 0.075 and 0.101 mag separately.  A virtually identical F160W result is quoted in the same paper. Why SBF and not the numbers this manifest adopted until now: at z = 0.0098 the Hubble flow is not a distance (the host's peculiar velocity alone is 307 +- 230 km/s against a flow of ~2900, which is what the z < 0.02 guard in snredshift/distance.py is for), and four electromagnetic determinations plus one gravitational one exist, all agreeing on 40-42 Mpc --\n  SBF   Cantiello+ 2018  40.7 +- 1.4 +- 1.9   <- adopted, most precise\n  GCLF  Lee et al. 2018, ApJL 859, L6, 41.65 +- 3.00\n  FP    Im et al. 2018, ApJL 855, L3, DOI 10.3847/2041-8213/aa9367, angular\n        diameter 37.7 +- 8.7 Mpc\n  combined redshift + FP  Hjorth et al. 2017, ApJL 848, L31,\n        DOI 10.3847/2041-8213/aa9110, \"The combined redshift and FP distance\n        is D_NGC4993 = 41.0 +- 3.1 Mpc\"\n  GW standard siren  Hjorth et al. 2017 Table 2, 43.8 +2.9/-6.9 Mpc\nThe previously adopted 41.0 +- 3.1 is the *least* precise of the four electromagnetic ones (0.164 mag of distance modulus against SBF's 0.125), and this manifest's own note called its error \"the largest single term in any shipped template's budget\" while quoting the better number as a cross-check. Changing it moves DM by -0.016 mag and the distance error term from 0.164 to 0.125 mag, and docs/VALIDATION.md section A checks that term as `V-A5` (the modulus and both printed error terms) and `V-A6` (the single number carried here) -- the two rows that are this distance in full, and that are the reason the quadrature sum is asserted in a test rather than only stated here. Still not a Hubble-flow distance and still not treated as one: the same Hjorth paper gives v_CMB = 3231 +- 53 km/s for the NGC 4993 group, four times the heliocentric recession rate, so what a redshift-derived distance would give here is mostly the group motion."
    },
