   "mu": {
    "engine": 32.8585,
    "engine_distance": {
     "d_L_Mpc": 37.3,
     "d_L_err_Mpc": 2.6,
     "kind": "redshift-velocity-field",
     "z": 0.0087,
     "z_err": null,
     "mu": 32.8585,
     "cosmology": null,
     "source": "The kind is `redshift-velocity-field` and not `measured`, which is the whole content of this paragraph.  Read off Clocchiatti et al. 2011, AJ 141, 163, DOI 10.1088/0004-6256/141/5/163, which is the publishing source of this table (see `provenance`): \"The distance to ESO 184-G082 was taken to be 37.3 +- 2.6 Mpc, as computed by the NED database from the redshift measured by Foley et al. 2006, a Hubble constant of 74.2 km/s/Mpc (Riess et al. 2009), and the model of the local velocity field given by Mould et al. 2000.\"  So this is a redshift-derived distance with a velocity-field correction, quoted with its error -- which is what makes the distance term of the error budget real rather than zero (2.6/37.3 = 7.0 % in fractional distance, i.e. 0.151 mag of distance modulus).  That 7 % is independently confirmed inside the same paper: both flux columns of its Table 3 carry the footnote \"Uncertainty does not include the ~7 % uncertainty in the distance\", which is the same 2.6/37.3 and the reason a distance error has to travel with the distance.  The host is the one this paper names, ESO 184-G82; the two catalogue strings v1 carried for it (ES024014.1-251413, E186-G210) were not reconciled against a catalogue in this build, so they are not repeated here."
    },
