   "mu": {
    "engine": 30.24,
    "engine_distance": {
     "d_L_Mpc": 11.168632477805588,
     "d_L_err_Mpc": 2.417372305337284,
     "kind": "measured-secondary",
     "z": 0.0022016,
     "z_err": null,
     "mu": 30.24,
     "cosmology": null,
     "source": "The kind is `measured-secondary` and not `measured`, and the difference is not cosmetic: `measured` means a primary distance indicator in the vocabulary `snredshift.distance` defines (TRGB, Cepheids, RR Lyrae, SBF, the fundamental plane, a standard siren), and a Tully-Fisher modulus is calibrated against those rather than independent of them, so its calibration uncertainty is already inside the quoted error.  No Cepheid or TRGB determination for M74 could be found in this round, so this is the best available *and* a secondary one; a caller who needs only primary determinations should filter on `kind`. Gal-Yam et al. 2002, MNRAS 332, L73, DOI 10.1046/j.1365-8711.2002.05535.x: \"Tully (1988), mu = 30.24 for H0 = 65\" -- a distance modulus for M74 adopted verbatim from that line.  mu_err is deliberately NOT a statistical error: half the gap to the other published modulus for this host -- GCN 1238 (Berger et al.) writes \"Given the distance modulus of M74 of 29.3\" -- so mu_err = (30.24 - 29.30)/2 = 0.47, a declared spread between two literature values (11.2 Mpc against 7.2 Mpc) rather than a sigma, and it is documented as such so no reader mistakes it for one.  The cosmological branch is not an option here and needs no allow_low_z flag: at z = 0.0022 the peculiar-velocity guard (0.02) refuses it outright, which is the guard doing its job -- 660 km/s of host/halo motion is not a Hubble-flow measurement, and a cosmological distance at this redshift would be the model pretending to be data."
    },
